{"type":"thinking","text":"Image looks quite good! But issues:\n1. Pelican body seems disconnected from saddle? It sits above? Actually body near seat, looks okay.\n2. Legs invisible in static preview? Wait I see maybe legs not rendered? Under body, between body and crank, there are no visible orange legs! The image shows nothing connecting body to pedals. Why? Hmm view full. I see a black line from saddle down? The legs might be hidden behind body? Near leg path starts at 462 306 and should extend down to 528,455, but static render shows no orange. Why? Maybe because path `d` attribute is inside `<animate>` and base path has no `d` attribute on `<path>`; in static rendering with no animation support, path has no d. rsvg-convert likely doesn't support SMIL so the path doesn't render. In browser it would at t=0. But accessibility/static thumbnail fails; image should always show a complete pelican even before animation. Need set base `d` on the paths and feet groups base transform? animateMotion may not render but feet maybe at 0,0? I don't see feet either. Actually in static image no orange legs/feet. The far foot group with animateMotion probably renders at origin (0,0) hidden top-left. So add base d attributes for leg paths; set foot base transform to start positions via `<g transform=\"translate(528 455)\">` with animateMotion additive? Need handle: if group has transform translate(528,455) and animateMotion path also translates, double. For static initial, need a transform, and for animation, animateMotion overrides/adds. Hmm animateMotion adds a translation to transform? It may replace? Spec: motion transform is post-multiplied to transform attribute? It composes. Let's recall: `<animateMotion>`'s transform is applied as a supplemental transformation matrix, post-multiplied to existing transform. So if base transform translate(528,455), animation path adds again -> double. Could use CSS? Better use nested groups:\n```\n<g transform=\"translate(528 455)\">\n <g>\